videos.script:
Open two apps you know well, one on Android and one on an iPhone, and look at where things sit. {{two}} The title, the button that adds something, the bar that switches sections. They are in different places, and on purpose.

Each platform publishes a design system. Google's is Material Design, and Apple's is the Human Interface Guidelines. {{guides}} They decide what a user expects before your app has said a word. Today you read the two side by side, on one screen of the study planner. {{screen}} Where the main action lives. Where the sections go. Which font the text uses, and what happens in dark mode. {{type}}

Then the question every team argues about. What belongs to your brand, and what belongs to the platform? {{brand}} You write the planner's colours, spacing and sizes once, as design tokens, and turn them into two themes. {{tokens}} The colours stay the same in both. The conventions move. By the end, you can say which is which.
